<div class="page">

  <header class="masthead">
    <div class="eyebrow">Build System V · 入門導覽</div>
    <h1>BSV 架構地圖</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">Six layers, from the command you type down to the bytes in the Nix store. Read it once top to bottom, then use it as a lookup table.</p>
  </header>

  <!-- ============ THE ONE IDEA ============ -->
  <section>
    <h2><span class="h2-num">00</span> 一句話</h2>
    <div class="thesis">
      <p class="lead">BSV keeps <b>one recipe per program</b>, and builds it many times — once for every machine that should run it. Nothing else in the repo is as important as this idea.</p>
      <div class="formula">
        1 package file&nbsp;<span class="op">×</span>&nbsp;( <span class="kw">build</span> , <span class="hostword">host</span> )&nbsp;<span class="op">=</span>&nbsp;1 derivation&nbsp;<span class="op">→</span>&nbsp;1 path in /nix/store
      </div>
      <p><code>pkgs/hello/default.nix</code> is one file. It is called 3 times with 3 different <code>host</code> values, so it produces 3 different <code>hello</code> binaries: one for Linux, one for Intel Mac, one for ARM Mac.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ VOCABULARY ============ -->
  <section>
    <h2><span class="h2-num">01</span> 四個字，先分清楚</h2>
    <p class="lead">Most of the confusion in BSV is these four words. They all describe a machine, but a different machine each time. Ask the question in bold and you always get the right one.</p>
    <div class="vocab">
      <div>
        <span class="word">system</span>
        <span class="q">Which machine runs the build job?</span>
        <p style="font-size:.88rem">A Nix concept. It decides <i>who is allowed to do the work</i>. BSV has 4.</p>
        <span class="ex">x86_64-linux · aarch64-linux<br>aarch64-darwin · x86_64-windows</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="word">build</span>
        <span class="q">Who runs the compiler?</span>
        <p style="font-size:.88rem">A BSV platform set. Tools in <code>root</code> come from here.</p>
        <span class="ex">almost always<br>x86_64-linux-gnu</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="word alt">host</span>
        <span class="q">Who runs the result?</span>
        <p style="font-size:.88rem">A BSV platform set. <code>CC</code>, <code>CFLAGS</code>, <code>prefix</code>, <code>triple</code> come from here.</p>
        <span class="ex">x86_64-linux-gnu (native)<br>x86_64-apple-darwin18<br>nds64le-elf-newlib-v5d</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="word alt">target</span>
        <span class="q">Who runs the code my compiler emits?</span>
        <p style="font-size:.88rem">Only compilers and debuggers need this third word. A normal package never sees it.</p>
        <span class="ex">pkgs/andes/toolchain.nix<br>{ bsv, build, target }</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p><b>build = host</b> is a native build. <b>build ≠ host</b> is a cross build. That is the whole story.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ THE SIX LAYERS ============ -->
  <section>
    <h2><span class="h2-num">02</span> 六層架構</h2>
    <p class="lead">Every layer has one job and one real file. When something confuses you, first ask: <i>which layer is this?</i></p>

    <div class="spine">

      <div class="layer">
        <div class="rail"><div class="node">1</div><div class="thread"></div></div>
        <div class="layer-body">
          <div class="layer-head">
            <h3>Environment</h3><span class="layer-zh">執行環境</span>
            <span class="path">bin/bsv · src/cli/</span>
          </div>
          <p><code>./bin/bsv env</code> starts a bash sub-shell and hides the differences between PCs. It mounts the repo at <code>/bsv</code>, and <code>/bsv/nix</code> at <code>/nix</code>. So every path is the same on every machine, no matter where you cloned the repo.</p>
          <p>The <code>bsv</code> commands are thin wrappers. <code>bsv build</code> is 6 lines of TypeScript that call <code>nix build</code>.</p>
          <p class="touch"><b>You touch this when</b> you run any command, or add a new sub-command.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="layer">
        <div class="rail"><div class="node">2</div><div class="thread"></div></div>
        <div class="layer-body">
          <div class="layer-head">
            <h3>Flake</h3><span class="layer-zh">整個 repo 的入口</span>
            <span class="path">flake.nix → default.nix</span>
          </div>
          <p><code>flake.nix</code> is 5 lines; it just imports <code>default.nix</code>. That file is the table of contents of BSV: <code>lib</code>, <code>legacyPackages</code> (the packages), <code>apps</code> (runnable tools), <code>checks</code> (CI), <code>reports</code> (the build report tables), <code>formatter</code>.</p>
          <p class="touch"><b>You touch this when</b> you add a whole new output — rarely.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="layer">
        <div class="rail"><div class="node">3</div><div class="thread"></div></div>
        <div class="layer-body">
          <div class="layer-head">
            <h3>System</h3><span class="layer-zh">哪台機器做工</span>
            <span class="path">pkgs/x86_64-linux.nix</span>
          </div>
          <p>One file per Nix system. <code>pkgs/x86_64-linux.nix</code> is the entry for your PC. It lists the platforms available there (layer 4), pulls a few tools from nixpkgs, and ends with one important line:</p>
          <pre><code>|&gt; (self: self // self.x86_64-linux-gnu)</code></pre>
          <p>That line copies everything from the native platform up to the top level. It is the reason <code>/bsv#verilator</code> works as a short name.</p>
          <p class="touch"><b>You touch this when</b> you add a new host platform.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="layer">
        <div class="rail"><div class="node">4</div><div class="thread"></div></div>
        <div class="layer-body">
          <div class="layer-head">
            <h3>Platform</h3><span class="layer-zh">build / host 本體</span>
            <span class="path">pkgs/linux-gnu.nix</span>
          </div>
          <p>This is the file that <i>is</i> the <code>build</code> and <code>host</code> argument. 1142 lines, in five parts:</p>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Part</th><th>What lives there</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td class="mono">19–51</td><td>platform attributes</td><td><code>name</code>, <code>prefix</code>, <code>triple</code>, <code>CC</code>, <code>CFLAGS</code>, <code>PATH</code> — the fields packages read</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="mono">53–146</td><td>base pieces</td><td>glibc headers, libz, ca-certificates, taken out of RockyLinux 8.4 RPMs</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="mono">148–412</td><td>bootstrap</td><td><code>stage0</code> → <code>stage1</code> → <code>stdenv</code>. 雞生蛋: you need a compiler to build a compiler</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="mono">414–908</td><td><code>### Packages</code></td><td>~120 packages, one line each. <code>hello</code> is line 551, <code>verilator</code> line 736, <code>andes</code> line 773</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="mono">909 / 1037</td><td>runners / tests</td><td>QEMU and SID run scripts, then the test suites that use them</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>Line 20–21 says <code>build = self; host = self;</code> — so every package listed here is a <b>native</b> build. The cross platforms are separate files (<code>pkgs/x86_64-apple-darwin18.nix</code>, <code>pkgs/x86_64-w64-mingw32.nix</code>, …) that set <code>host</code> to themselves and <code>build</code> to Linux.</p>
          <p class="touch"><b>You touch this when</b> you register your new package — this is the file you edit.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="layer">
        <div class="rail"><div class="node">5</div><div class="thread"></div></div>
        <div class="layer-body">
          <div class="layer-head">
            <h3>Package</h3><span class="layer-zh">一個軟體的食譜</span>
            <span class="path">pkgs/&lt;name&gt;/default.nix</span>
          </div>
          <p>A function that takes <code>{ bsv, build, host ? build }</code> and returns a derivation. Four questions, always in this order:</p>
          <pre><code><span class="kw">name</span>    = "${build.system}.${host.name}.hello-2.12";
                                <span class="cm"># what to call it</span>
<span class="kw">root</span>    = [ build.stdenv-nocc … ];   <span class="cm"># which tools go in the sandbox</span>
<span class="kw">env</span>     = { src = …; CC = …; };     <span class="cm"># inputs + shell variables</span>
<span class="kw">builder</span> = lib.configureMake { … }; <span class="cm"># the commands to run</span></code></pre>
          <p><code>host ? build</code> means "if nobody gives me a <code>host</code>, I am a native build".</p>
          <p class="touch"><b>You touch this when</b> you add or update a program. This is 95% of the work in BSV.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="layer">
        <div class="rail"><div class="node">6</div><div class="thread"></div></div>
        <div class="layer-body">
          <div class="layer-head">
            <h3>Builder</h3><span class="layer-zh">真正動手的人</span>
            <span class="path">lib/*.nix + src/builders/*.ts</span>
          </div>
          <p>Two halves. <code>lib/</code> is the Nix half — it writes the recipe. <code>src/builders/</code> is the runtime half — it does the work.</p>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th>Half</th><th>File</th><th>Job</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Nix</td><td class="mono">lib/bwrap-derivation.nix</td><td>wraps your attributes into a real derivation, sets builder = <code>bwrap-builder@v0</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Nix</td><td class="mono">lib/configure-make.nix<br>lib/cmake-builder.nix<br>lib/meson-builder.nix<br>lib/bash-builder.nix</td><td>generate the bash script. <code>configureMake</code> is literally <code>$src/configure</code> + <code>make</code> + <code>make install DESTDIR=$out</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Nix</td><td class="mono">lib/fetch-tarball.nix<br>lib/fetch-git.nix</td><td>get the source; both need a hash or a <code>rev</code>, so the build is reproducible</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Runtime</td><td class="mono">src/builders/bwrap-builder.ts</td><td>merges every path in <code>root</code> into one directory, binds it as <code>/</code>, runs the script in <code>/build</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Runtime</td><td class="mono">src/builders/msys2-builder.ts</td><td>boots a Windows VM over QEMU and builds inside it via SSH</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>Only six builders are allowed; <code>bsv check</code> rejects anything else (<code>src/cli/commands/check.ts:126</code>). This is why every package looks the same.</p>
          <p class="touch"><b>You touch this when</b> a package needs a build style that does not exist yet — rarely.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ RESOLUTION CHAIN ============ -->
  <section>
    <h2><span class="h2-num">03</span> 一個命令，走完六層</h2>
    <p class="lead">This is the same six layers again, but now as one command. <code>bsv build /bsv#verilator</code>:</p>
    <div class="chain">
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-label">Layer 1</div>
        <div>
          <div class="step-what">bsv build /bsv#verilator&nbsp; →&nbsp; nix build --print-build-logs --no-link --print-out-paths /bsv#verilator</div>
          <div class="step-note">The CLI adds flags and steps aside. <code>src/cli/commands/build.ts</code></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-label">Layer 2</div>
        <div>
          <div class="step-what"><b>/bsv</b> = flake ref (the directory holding flake.nix) &nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>#</b> = separator &nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>verilator</b> = attribute path</div>
          <div class="step-note">Nix tries <code>packages.&lt;system&gt;.verilator</code>, then <code>legacyPackages.&lt;system&gt;.verilator</code>. BSV only has the second.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-label">Layer 3</div>
        <div>
          <div class="step-what">legacyPackages.<b>x86_64-linux</b>.verilator</div>
          <div class="step-note">Your PC's system. The short name resolves because of the <code>self // self.x86_64-linux-gnu</code> alias line.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-label">Layer 4</div>
        <div>
          <div class="step-what">…<b>x86_64-linux-gnu</b>.verilator&nbsp; ←&nbsp; pkgs/linux-gnu.nix:736</div>
          <div class="step-note">The platform passes itself in as <code>build</code>. No <code>host</code> given, so host = build → native.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-label">Layer 5</div>
        <div>
          <div class="step-what">pkgs/verilator/default.nix&nbsp; →&nbsp; /nix/store/…-x86_64-linux.x86_64-linux-gnu.verilator-5.046.drv</div>
          <div class="step-note">Evaluation finishes here. The <code>.drv</code> file is the recipe in its final form. Nothing has compiled yet.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-label">Layer 6</div>
        <div>
          <div class="step-what">bwrap sandbox&nbsp; →&nbsp; autoconf · ./configure · make · make installbin installdata&nbsp; →&nbsp; $out</div>
          <div class="step-note">Or: the cache server already has it, and nothing is built at all — just downloaded.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ HOSTS ============ -->
  <section>
    <h2><span class="h2-num">04</span> Host 清單</h2>
    <p class="lead">Three groups. Group A is other computers; group B is Andes RISC-V chips. Only the native one has a short name.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Host</th><th>Result runs on</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td rowspan="4">A · 別的電腦</td><td class="mono">x86_64-linux-gnu</td><td>your Linux PC (native)</td><td class="mono">/bsv#hello</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">x86_64-apple-darwin18</td><td>Intel Mac</td><td class="mono">/bsv#x86_64-apple-darwin18.hello</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">aarch64-apple-darwin20</td><td>Apple Silicon Mac</td><td class="mono">/bsv#aarch64-apple-darwin20.hello</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">x86_64-w64-mingw32<br>x86_64-msys<br>x86_64-none</td><td>Windows exe · MSYS2 · bare-metal x86</td><td class="mono">/bsv#x86_64-w64-mingw32.gmp</td></tr>
          <tr>
            <td>B · RISC-V<br>(25 個)</td>
            <td class="mono">nds{32,64}le-{elf,linux}-<br>{newlib,mculib,glibc,musl}-v5{,e,f,d}</td>
            <td>Andes RISC-V cores. <code>e</code> = RV32E, <code>f</code> = single float, <code>d</code> = double float</td>
            <td class="mono">/bsv#nds64le-elf-newlib-v5d.libnn</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>C · 工具本體</td>
            <td class="mono">andes.&lt;toolchain&gt;</td>
            <td>Not a host — the cross compiler itself, which runs on your PC</td>
            <td class="mono">/bsv#andes.nds64le-elf-newlib-v5d</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Group B comes from <code>pkgs/andes/toolchain-configs.nix</code>, expanded into hosts at <code>pkgs/x86_64-linux.nix:7</code>. The arch string is built there too, for example <code>rv32imc_zicsr_zifencei_zalrsc_zca_xandescodense_xandesperf</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ REPO MAP ============ -->
  <section>
    <h2><span class="h2-num">05</span> Repo 目錄地圖</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Directory</th><th>Layer</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="mono">pkgs/</td><td>4 + 5</td><td>Platforms and package recipes. <code>pkgs/andes/</code> is the Andes-specific half</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">lib/</td><td>6</td><td>The Nix helper library: derivations, fetchers, build scripts</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">src/</td><td>1 + 6</td><td>TypeScript (Deno). <code>cli/</code> = the <code>bsv</code> commands, <code>builders/</code> = the sandbox runtimes, <code>ci/</code>, <code>report/</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">bin/</td><td>1</td><td>Bundled tools: <code>nix</code>, <code>bwrap</code>, <code>deno</code>, <code>bash</code> — so BSV does not use your PC's versions</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">tests/</td><td>—</td><td>Test suites: <code>hello</code>, <code>gcc</code>, <code>glibc</code>, <code>coremark-v5</code>, <code>dhrystone</code>, <code>eembc</code>, <code>plumhall</code>, <code>boot-linux</code>…</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">reports/</td><td>—</td><td>The tables shown on the build report web page. One <code>.nix</code> per report</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">docs/</td><td>—</td><td>mdbook source for the official documentation</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ ADD A PACKAGE ============ -->
  <section>
    <h2><span class="h2-num">06</span> 加一個自己的 package</h2>
    <p class="lead">Only layers 4 and 5 are involved. Copy <code>pkgs/hello/default.nix</code> — it is the smallest complete example in the tree.</p>

    <div class="recipe">
      <div class="rstep"><div class="rstep-body">
        <h3>Copy the smallest example</h3>
        <pre><code>mkdir pkgs/mypkg
cp pkgs/hello/default.nix pkgs/mypkg/default.nix</code></pre>
      </div></div>

      <div class="rstep"><div class="rstep-body">
        <h3>Fix the name — <code>bsv check</code> is strict about it</h3>
        <p>The <code>pname</code> must be exactly the attribute path. Attribute <code>x86_64-linux-gnu.mypkg</code> therefore needs:</p>
        <pre><code>name = "${build.system}.${host.name}.mypkg-1.0";</code></pre>
      </div></div>

      <div class="rstep"><div class="rstep-body">
        <h3>Point <code>src</code> at your source</h3>
        <p>Tarball, with the hash generated for you:</p>
        <pre><code>echo '    url = "https://example.com/mypkg-1.0.tar.gz";' | bsv gen-hash</code></pre>
        <p>Or git, which needs an exact <code>rev</code> — see <code>pkgs/verilator/default.nix</code> for the pattern, including the <code>cp --archive $src/. .</code> step that a git tree needs.</p>
      </div></div>

      <div class="rstep"><div class="rstep-body">
        <h3>Register it in the platform</h3>
        <p>In <code>pkgs/linux-gnu.nix</code>, inside <code>### Packages</code>, in alphabetical order:</p>
        <pre><code>mypkg = import ./mypkg/default.nix {
  inherit bsv;
  inherit build;
};</code></pre>
      </div></div>

      <div class="rstep"><div class="rstep-body">
        <h3>Build, format, check</h3>
        <pre><code>bsv build /bsv#mypkg
ls "$(bsv build /bsv#mypkg)"
bsv fmt
bsv check</code></pre>
        <p>Commit message style: <code>pkgs/mypkg: add mypkg</code> — scope, colon, lowercase imperative.</p>
      </div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ CHEATSHEET ============ -->
  <section>
    <h2><span class="h2-num">07</span> 常用命令</h2>
    <div class="cheats">
      <div class="cheat"><span class="cmd">./bin/bsv env</span><span class="why">Enter the BSV environment. Everything else needs this first.</span></div>
      <div class="cheat"><span class="cmd">bsv build /bsv#&lt;name&gt;</span><span class="why">Build, and print the store path.</span></div>
      <div class="cheat"><span class="cmd">bsv list-derivations /bsv#…</span><span class="why">See which names you are allowed to type.</span></div>
      <div class="cheat"><span class="cmd">nix eval /bsv#legacyPackages.x86_64-linux --apply builtins.attrNames</span><span class="why">List the hosts and top-level attributes.</span></div>
      <div class="cheat"><span class="cmd">bsv build-shell /bsv#&lt;name&gt;</span><span class="why">Drop into the same sandbox the build uses — best way to debug a failing build.</span></div>
      <div class="cheat"><span class="cmd">bsv fmt &nbsp;·&nbsp; bsv check</span><span class="why">Format, then verify naming, builders and evaluation. Run both before every commit.</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

